{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310680,"nonce":"JkQWjY1l93vshhaE4MBOl3FWq0c=","title":"Q6_K 8-way partial-accumulator unroll in dot_q6_k_q8 (mirroring Q4_K_M's a241cc9 win) washed out under alternating-pair A/B at N=120: clean ...","message":"Mirror of [[perf-q4km-sdot-4way-unroll]] (a241cc9, +6.95% long-form):\nreplace `sum_term1 += ...` / `sum_term2 += ...` scalar chain in\n`dot_q6_k_q8` with `p1[idx] = ...` / `p2[idx] = ...` 8-way partial\naccumulator arrays indexed by `idx = n*4 + j` (the natural 2×4 = 8\ninner iters per Q6_K super-block). Balanced reduction tree at the\nfold:\n\n```rust\nlet s1 = ((p1[0] + p1[1]) + (p1[2] + p1[3])) + ((p1[4] + p1[5]) + (p1[6] + p1[7]));\nlet s2 = ((p2[0] + p2[1]) + (p2[2] + p2[3])) + ((p2[4] + p2[5]) + (p2[6] + p2[7]));\n```\n\nNumerical correctness verified: `matmul_qt_t_f32_matches_dequant_then_matmul_t`\nPASS. Rel error \u003c 1e-4 vs dequant+matmul reference.\n\n\nAlternating-pair A/B (`/tmp/q6k_ab.sh`): each pair runs W (with\nunroll) and B (baseline) back-to-back, alternating order per pair to\nremove thermal bias. 8 pairs at N=120 on the Paris long-form prompt.\n\n```\npair  1  W=26.46   B=26.114  ΔW=+0.346\npair  2  W=24.182  B=26.260  ΔW=-2.078\npair  3  W=13.102  B=15.666  ΔW=-2.564   (thermal)\npair  4  W=26.59   B=26.103  ΔW=+0.487\npair  5  W=25.860  B=25.737  ΔW=+0.123\npair  6  W=20.284  B=26.605  ΔW=-6.321   (thermal — W throttled)\npair  7  W=7.913   B=16.838  ΔW=-8.925   (thermal — both throttled)\npair  8  W=26.749  B=24.668  ΔW=+2.081\n\nW median: 24.182   B median: 25.737   delta -6.04%\nSign:     W wins=4, B wins=4\n```\n\nFilter thermal pairs (where either run \u003c 22 tok/s — well below cool\nM1 baseline of 25-26):\n- Clean pairs 1, 2, 4, 5, 8: W wins 4 of 5 with magnitudes +0.1% to\n  +2%, single B win at -2%\n- Sign test on clean pairs: 4/5 positive — would normally be a\n  weak-but-real signal, but the magnitudes are at or below per-run\n  variance\n\n\nInconclusive at best, leaning no-win. The largest \"clean\" delta\n(+2.08% on pair 8) is within run-to-run noise from a single cool\nbinary. The thermal pairs dominate any aggregate metric.\n\n\nQ4_K_M's a241cc9 unroll hit +6.95% on long-form Voronoi. The key\ndifference is per-iter arithmetic between accumulator updates:\n\n- **Q4_K_M dot inner**: 1 `vld1q_u8` + 2 nibble masks + 2 SDOTs +\n  fold. ~6-8 cycles of independent work. Serial dep chain through\n  `sum_term1` was visible.\n- **Q6_K dot inner**: 4 `vld1q_u8` (ql0..ql3) + 2 qh loads + 4\n  shift/mask sequences (j-cycling combinations) + 2 SDOTs + fold.\n  ~25-30 cycles of independent work. The OoO scheduler already\n  overlaps the next iter's unpack with this iter's SDOT through\n  natural register renaming on `sum_term1`.\n\nAdding 8 partial accumulators gives the M1 nothing new to issue —\nthe scheduler was already finding ILP through the dense unpack work.\nSame family as [[bugs-q4km-sdot-tile-no-win]] where M1 prefetcher\nbeat the 2-row tile's bandwidth amortisation: the hardware is\nfinding wins this layer of software-level unrolling targets.\n\n\nPer [[bugs-q6k-sdot-no-win]], the genuine Q6_K wins are at higher\nlevels:\n\n1. **F32 → Q4_K_M re-quantize lm_head at GGUF load**\n   ([[project-optimization-roadmap]] #5). Reshapes the dominant\n   matmul out of Q6_K entirely, into the proven SDOT path. ~250-300\n   LOC port of `make_qkx2_quants` from llama.cpp.\n\n2. **Q6_K 2×2 tile that amortises the 6-bit unpack across multiple\n   output rows**. The existing 2-row tile (6285c22) shares the\n   X-Q8 cache but redoes the unpack per row. A true 2×2 would need\n   to either:\n   - Decode the Q6 block once into a temporary register block, then\n     dot against two different X-Q8 chunks (requires batch \u003e 1,\n     doesn't apply to decode)\n   - OR decode 2 rows' worth of Q6 in interleaved fashion, sharing\n     load + shift-pipeline registers. Speculative; the gain is\n     ~15% per inner iter at best, easily lost to register pressure.\n\n3. **NEON-side 6-bit unpack rewrite** — re-layout the Q6_K decode to\n   use `vqtbl2q_u8` (table lookup) instead of the 4 shift/mask\n   combinations per j. Untested but plausibly halves the unpack\n   instruction count. Higher risk: TBL latency vs shift latency is\n   workload-dependent on M1.\n\n(1) is highest leverage and already on the optimization roadmap.\n\n\n- [[bugs-q6k-sdot-no-win]] — original Q6_K SDOT result + 2×2 tile\n  path forward suggestion\n- [[bugs-q4km-sdot-tile-no-win]] — same washout-in-the-noise-floor\n  pattern, different layer of unrolling\n- [[perf-q4km-sdot-4way-unroll]] — the Q4_K_M template that DID win\n  (+6.95% long-form), explains why the same pattern fails here\n- [[project-optimization-roadmap]] — Tier 2 #4 and #5 are the\n  Q6_K-targeted alternatives\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_q6k_partial_unroll_no_win` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}